    "markdown": "## Present continuous: Form\n\n![Grammar chart showing the form of the Present Continuous tense in positive, negative, and question forms, including short answers and spelling rules for -ing words.](/uploads/grammar/a1/present-continuous/img-1.png)\n\nWe form the present continuous with **am/is/are** + the **\\-ing** form of the verb.\n\n-   *I **am writing** a book.*\n-   *She **is listening** to the radio.* \n-   *They **are doing** their homework.* \n\nThe short forms of **[am/is/are](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/present-simple-forms-of-to-be/)** are: **‘m/’s/’re**.\n\n-   *I**‘m writing** a book.*\n-   *She**‘s listening** to the radio.* \n-   *They**‘re doing** their homework.* \n\nThe negative short forms of **am not/is not/are not** are: **‘m not/isn’t/aren’t**.\n\n-   *I**‘m not writing** a book.*\n-   *She **isn’t listening** to the radio.* \n-   *They **aren’t doing** their homework.* \n\n❗ **Note** that we can use **short forms only in negative short answers**, but not in positive short answers.\n\n-   *‘Are you writing a book?’ ‘No, **I’m not/I am not**.’ ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/present-continuous/img-2.png)* \n-   *‘Are you writing a book?’ ‘Yes, **I am**.’* *![](/uploads/grammar/a1/present-continuous/img-3.png)*\n-   *‘Are you writing a book?’ ‘Yes, **I’m**.’* ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/present-continuous/img-4.png)\n\n#### Actions happening now\n\nWe use the present continuous for things happening now, at the moment of speaking.\n\n-   *‘What **are** you **doing**?’ ‘I**‘m watching** TV.’*\n\n#### Actions happening around now\n\nWe use the present continuous for temporary things that are happening around now (=not at the moment of speaking but these days, or weeks, etc.)\n\n-   *I**‘m reading** a very good book at the moment.* (=I’m not reading it at the moment I’m speaking, but I’m reading it these days.)\n\n#### **Now, these days, at the moment, etc.**\n\nWe use the present continuous with [time expressions](https://test-english.com/vocabulary/a1/time-words-a1-english-vocabulary/) that mean ‘now’ or ‘around now’: **now, at the moment, at present, today, these days, this week, etc**.",
